.TH "ZE" "1" "June 2026" "ze" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ze \- pattern-based navigation of a frecency-ranked dynamic directory stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ze [ \-cefhlrt ] [ args ]
.SH AVAILABILITY
bash, zsh, ksh93, mksh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.P
\fBze.sh\fR is a directory jumper for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, \fBksh93\fR, and
\fBmksh\fR. Unlike its predecessor \fBz.sh\fR, \fBze.sh\fR uses an \fBexponential
moving sum (EMS)\fR scoring model on an "\fBevent clock\fR" (a global monotonic
event counter shared across all stored entries) and only tracks navigation
performed through the \fBze\fR command itself (or \fBcd\fR if aliased to
\fB_ze_cd\fR). After a short learning phase, \fBze\fR will take you to the
highest-scoring directory that matches the given pattern(s).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR Restrict matches to subdirectories of the current directory.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR Print the best match, do not \fBcd\fR.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR Use \fBfzf\fR for interactive selection (if \fBfzf\fR is installed).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR Show a brief help message.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR List all matching directories by score.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR Sort matches by visit count only.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR Sort matches by recency of last visit only.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP 14
\fBze\fR Change to \fB$HOME\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze \-\fR Change to the previous directory.
.TP 14
\fBze foo\fR Change to the highest-scoring directory matching \fBfoo\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze foo bar\fR Change to the highest-scoring directory matching both \fBfoo\fR
and \fBbar\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze -c foo\fR Change to the highest-scoring subdirectory of \fB$PWD\fR matching
\fBfoo\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze -l foo\fR List all directories matching \fBfoo\fR, sorted by score.
.TP 14
\fBze -r foo\fR Change to the most frequently visited directory matching
\fBfoo\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze -t foo\fR Change to the most recently visited directory matching \fBfoo\fR.
.TP 14
\fBze -f foo\fR Interactively select a matching directory using \fBfzf\fR.
.SH INSTALLATION
.P
Put the following in your shell rc file (\fB.bashrc\fR, \fB.kshrc\fR, etc.):
.RS
.P
\fBsource /path/to/ze.sh\fR
.RE
.P
Optionally, to track ordinary \fBcd\fR commands as well:
.RS
.P
\fBalias cd=_ze_cd\fR
.RE
.P
Use \fBze\fR and aliased \fBcd\fR with pathname-based navigation for some time to
build up the database. Afterwards use
.B ze
for pattern-based navigation of the directories known to the database.
.SH DESIGN
.P
\fBze.sh\fR differs from \fBz.sh\fR in several ways:
.RS
.TP
\fBevent clock\fR
\fBze.sh\fR uses an event clock (each newly recorded \fBcd\fR event
advances the clock by one tick) instead of wall-clock time for score decay. This
prevents score decay during shell inactivity, especially during long periods (e.g.
holidays).
.TP
\fBscoring\fR \fBze.sh\fR uses an \fBexponential moving sum (EMS)\fR scoring
model. The score is the sum of exponentially decayed unit impulses at each visit
time. The decay rate is controlled by \fB_ZE_LAMBDA\fR (default: \fB8e-3\fR).
.TP
\fBtracking\fR \fBze.sh\fR only tracks explicit \fBze\fR-based directory changes
(or bare \fBcd\fR if aliased to \fB_ze_cd\fR). It does not use precommand hooks.
.RE
.SH DATABASE
.P
Data is stored in \fB~/.ze/ze.db\fR as a plain text file with one entry per line:
.P
\fBpath|visits|ticks|score\fR
.P
Where:
.TP
\fBpath\fR
Absolute directory path.
.TP
\fBvisits\fR
Cumulative visit count for this entry.
.TP
\fBticks\fR
Global cumulative \fBcd\fR event count at time of last visit.
.TP
\fBscore\fR
Exponentially decayed cumulative visit score as of the last visit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.P
The following environment variables can be set (e.g., in your shell rc file):
.TP
\fB_ZE_CMD\fR
Command name (default: \fBze\fR).
.TP
\fB_ZE_DIR\fR
Database directory (default: \fB~/.ze\fR).
.TP
\fB_ZE_LAMBDA\fR
Decay constant in units of 1/\fBcd\fR action (default: \fB8e-3\fR).
.TP
\fB_ZE_DBMAX\fR
Database size limit (default: \fB512\fR).
.TP
\fB_ZE_OWNER\fR
Allow use on shared databases by specifying the owner username.
.TP
\fB_ZE_RESOLVE_SYMLINKS\fR
Set to any non-empty value to resolve symlinks to physical paths (default: logical
paths are honoured).
.TP
\fB_ZE_EXCLUDE_DIRS\fR Array of directory trees to exclude from tracking.
.SH FZF INTEGRATION
.P
If \fBfzf\fR is installed, \fBze -f [pattern]\fR opens an interactive selector
showing all matching directories ranked by score, with the best match at the top
of the \fBfzf\fR display.
.SH MIGRATING FROM Z.SH
.P
If you have an existing \fB~/.z\fR database from \fBz.sh\fR, you can convert it
for use with \fBze.sh\fR:
.RS
.P
\fBmkdir -p ~/.ze\fR
.P
\fBnow=$(awk -F'|' '{ now += $2 } END { print now }' ~/.z)\fR
.P
\fBsort -t'|' -k3,3n ~/.z | awk -F'|' -v now="$now" '\fR
.RS
.P
\fBBEGIN { OFS="|"; lambda=8e-3 }\fR
.P
\fB{   ticks += $2\fR
.P
\fB    r = exp(-lambda)\fR
.P
\fB    score = $2 == 1 ? 1 : (1 - r^$2) / (1 - r)\fR
.P
\fB    score *= exp(-lambda * (now - ticks))\fR
.P
\fB    print $1, $2, ticks, score\fR
.P
\fB}' > ~/.ze/ze.db\fR
.RE
.RE
.SH FILES
.P
\fB~/.ze/ze.db\fR \- Default database file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.P
A function \fB_ze\fR is defined, and the command specified by \fB_ZE_CMD\fR
(default: \fBze\fR) is aliased to it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.P
regex(7), pushd, popd, cdargs
.P
Project repository: https://github.com/jghub/ze
.SH NOTES
.TP
\fBSmartcase matching\fR Pattern matching is case-insensitive unless the pattern
contains uppercase characters.
.TP
\fBStale entries\fR Entries for directories that no longer exist are retained in
the database and filtered at match time. They may be pruned if the database
exceeds the size limit.
.TP
\fBSymlinks\fR By default, logical paths are honoured. Set
\fB_ZE_RESOLVE_SYMLINKS\fR to resolve symlinks to physical paths.
